10:33:04 | INFO | 🤖 Target: People often say “it’s stress” because stress is a common, understandable contributor—and for some people, clenching does get worse during stressful periods. But that shorthand skips the mechanism. A more accurate version is: **Stress can make your sleeping nervous system more prone to brief arousals and muscle bursts; in some people, those bursts show up as jaw clenching/grinding.** It is not that stress somehow travels specifically into the jaw. The brain already has circuits that control jaw position, chewing, swallowing, and airway-related movements. When sleep becomes lighter or more fragmented, those circuits may fire more often or more strongly. Your personal wiring, habits, sleep quality, medications/substances, and sometimes breathing issues affect whether that looks like clenching, leg movement, waking up, tossing and turning, or nothing noticeable. So “stress causes it” is not exactly wrong. It is just incomplete in the same way “stress causes headaches” is incomplete: stress can raise the odds, but it does not explain why the symptom appears in one body part rather than another, or why it persists in some people after stress improves. Also, plenty of people who grind have no obvious stress, and plenty of highly stressed people do not grind. That is why dentists and sleep clinicians do not treat sleep bruxism as purely psychological.
